window.STUDY_SEARCH=[{"title":"What creates a shared identity","chapter":"Visual language","href":"01-visual-language.html#what-creates-a-shared-identity","text":"Anthropic's public websites repeatedly combine pale backgrounds, dark text, serif headings, and restrained controls. Warm off-white surfaces, expressive letterforms, and generous space create a visual character close to books and magazines. The density and decoration change with the task. Company pages emphasize narrative and imagery, documentation prioritizes text and orientation, and the developer blog adds monospace lettering and pixel graphics."},{"title":"Three visual directions","chapter":"Visual language","href":"01-visual-language.html#three-visual-directions","text":"Direction Visible characteristics Main examples Design reading Warm editorial expression Off-white backgrounds, large serif type, open spacing, illustration, image-and-text composition Company, product, and resources Organizes technical product stories like a publication, with room for an introductory narrative Restrained knowledge interfaces Neutral colors, fine dividers, stable navigation areas, compact controls Platform, Code Docs, Academy, support Places finding, reading, and taking the next step at the center Retro developer expression Monospace labels, pixel graphics, straighter corners, article rows claude.dev Gives developer content its own identity while retaining familiar backgrounds and type families Sources: company S01, resources S04, Platform S08, developer blog S10."},{"title":"The roles of color","chapter":"Visual language","href":"01-visual-language.html#the-roles-of-color","text":"Measured backgrounds and text colors from the company, resources, and documentation pages The diagram collects measured background and text colors. The corresponding elements are listed below. Page and element Measured value Role Company and Claude Resources page background #FAF9F5 Adds a slight warmth to large empty areas Base text on those pages #141413 Gives body text and primary information a dark anchor Resources hero description #30302E Creates a small difference in emphasis from the heading Platform documentation background #FCFCFB Provides a near-white surface for dense reading Platform base text #0B0B0B Supplies the main dark tone for text and controls Platform introductory description #52514E Separates supporting text from the heading Evidence: S01 measurements, S04 measurements, S08 measurements. Color also has distinct roles within a page. The orange-brown brand mark provides recognition, pale pink and green enrich illustrations, and syntax colors help distinguish code structure."},{"title":"Space and visual rhythm","chapter":"Visual language","href":"01-visual-language.html#space-and-visual-rhythm","text":"Claude Resources with article cards around a central heading, description, and search field Figure 1 · Claude Resources · 1440 × 1000 · Source S04. The opening screen has three layers. Peripheral cards suggest the range of content; the central heading and description establish the topic; the search field turns attention into an available action. A faint grid connects the composition without dominating the text. Documentation assigns more space to navigation, articles, and code, creating a tighter reading rhythm. Space follows the page's task: the resources hero isolates a topic, while documentation separates navigation, text, and supporting tools."},{"title":"Illustrations, icons, and imagery","chapter":"Visual language","href":"01-visual-language.html#illustrations-icons-and-imagery","text":"Form Visible treatment Role in reading Line illustrations Outlines of books, people, or objects with soft areas of color Distinguish content and break up large stretches of text Pixel graphics Characters and article imagery on the developer blog Establish a technical, game-influenced tone Product demonstrations Tasks and outcomes on the product page Connect abstract capabilities to concrete situations Small interface icons Search, copy, contents, and arrows Identify actions; some sites use the Anthropicons font Developer blog with pixel imagery, article rows, and monospace labels Figure 2 · claude.dev · Source S10. Pixel imagery and monospace labels work together as its visual identity."},{"title":"Borders, corners, and shadows","chapter":"Visual language","href":"01-visual-language.html#borders-corners-and-shadows","text":"Cards often use pale borders and small background changes to form groups; primary buttons use solid dark fills. Resource cards, pricing cards, code boxes, and blog controls use different corner shapes and sizes for their respective tasks. The recurring division of labor is clear: borders group content, fills establish action priority, and space expresses relationships between sections."},{"title":"Five principal names, four core families","chapter":"Typography","href":"02-typography.html#five-principal-names-four-core-families","text":"The principal text fonts below were checked against page styles, loaded font records, and the fonts actually rendering sampled text. Name Category Main role Evidence Anthropic Sans Sans serif Body text, navigation, buttons, card descriptions S04, S05, S08 Anthropic Serif Serif Brand headings, page headings, some card titles S01, S04, S05 Anthropic Serif Display Display serif variant Claude Code documentation headings S07 Anthropic Mono Monospace Platform code, developer blog name and metadata S08, S10 Paper Mono Monospace Code blocks in Claude Code documentation S07, code verification This gives five principal names, or four core families when Serif Display is grouped with Serif. Weights, italics, variable versions, and fallback aliases are grouped with their corresponding families."},{"title":"Three typographic roles","chapter":"Typography","href":"02-typography.html#three-typographic-roles","text":"Headings establish tone, body text supports reading, and monospace text expresses technical structure. A type category can also cross roles: the company homepage combines sans and serif text in one heading, while the developer blog uses monospace for its name. The company homepage mixes sans-serif and serif text within its main statement Figure 3 · Company homepage S01. The opening of the main statement uses sans serif; the longer continuation uses serif. The area below shows one frame of a dynamic presentation. Changes in letterform and weight bring a short claim and a longer explanation into one composition. The browser's rendering records identify both Sans and Serif within that heading. The developer blog uses Anthropic Mono for its name, connecting the site's identity with the technical tone of its categories and dates."},{"title":"Measured type hierarchy","chapter":"Typography","href":"02-typography.html#measured-type-hierarchy","text":"These measurements use a 1440 × 1000 desktop viewport. Size and line height are in CSS pixels. Page and role Size Line height Weight Source Claude Resources main heading 72 79.2 500 S04 Resources hero description 23 34.5 400 S04 Pricing main heading 64 70.4 500 S03 Academy home main heading 48 52.8 400 S05 Academy home description 18 28 400 S05 Academy lesson heading 36 54 400 S06 Code Docs main heading 36 40 400 S07 Code Docs introduction 16 26.4 400 S07 Code Docs code 14 24 400 S07 Platform documentation heading 26.25 32.8125 520 S08 Developer blog name 31.5 34.65 400 S10 Marketing and resource entrances use larger headings; documentation makes more room for continuous text. Resources uses a 72px heading, compared with 26.25px on the sampled Platform page. The table retains actual variable-font weights, including 520, to preserve these differences."},{"title":"Two documentation sites, two code fonts","chapter":"Typography","href":"02-typography.html#two-documentation-sites-two-code-fonts","text":"Claude Code commands with copy controls and installation tabs Figure 4 · Code Docs S07. Paper Mono in this code area differs from Anthropic Mono in Platform documentation. Both sites separate natural language from code. The transferable pattern is the clear boundary between the two, with copy controls and platform choices close to the content they affect."},{"title":"Declared, loaded, and rendered fonts","chapter":"Typography","href":"02-typography.html#declared-loaded-and-rendered-fonts","text":"Layer What is checked Evidence Declaration Preferred and fallback fonts assigned to an element Element styles and font rules Loading Font resources loaded by the browser Font loading records Rendering The source of the glyphs in sampled text Browser font records for the element The support center's declaration differs from its captured rendering. It declares aliases for Anthropic Sans and Anthropic Serif, plus JetBrains Mono for a keyboard hint. In the captured sample, headings and body text fell back to Times New Roman, and the hint fell back to Consolas. The screenshot shows this fallback state. JetBrains Mono is therefore recorded as a local declaration, and Times New Roman and Consolas as fallback fonts. They are separate from the core-font count above. See renderedFonts and fontRuleSources in S09."},{"title":"Aliases and non-text fonts","chapter":"Typography","href":"02-typography.html#aliases-and-non-text-fonts","text":"Name in the sample Treatment in the study anthropicSans, anthropic-sans, Anthropic Sans Anthropic Sans anthropicSerif, anthropic-serif Anthropic Serif anthropicMono, Anthropic Mono Anthropic Mono Names containing Fallback, and backup fonts such as Arial or Georgia Separate from core brand-font counts Anthropicons-Variable Icon font, separate from text fonts Text, Web, Variable, Light, and similar file descriptors Retained in evidence and grouped with the corresponding family"},{"title":"Applying the typography to Chinese","chapter":"Typography","href":"02-typography.html#applying-the-typography-to-chinese","text":"The source samples are predominantly English. Chinese typography needs its own character-coverage check and real content to assess punctuation, weight, mixed scripts, and line breaks. This preserves hierarchy while adapting to different character density and reading rhythms."},{"title":"Grouping pages by task","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#grouping-pages-by-task","text":"Documentation on different domains can share a structure, while one Claude domain can contain product, pricing, and resource pages. These eight categories follow the reader's main task and show how content and actions are arranged around it. Structural diagrams for presentation, documentation, and article-list layouts A layout comparison: presentation pages, reading pages, and article lists assign space differently."},{"title":"At a glance","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#at-a-glance","text":"Type Reader's main question Structure Sample 1 Company and brand What does this company focus on? Statement, highlights, articles, company navigation S01 2 Product introduction What can this product help me do? Heading and action, scenarios, capability explanations, FAQ S02 3 Pricing comparison Which plan fits me? Audience selection, plan cards, comparison, explanations S03 4 Resource library Where is the content I need? Categories, search, filters, cards, topic groups S04 5 Technical documentation How do I complete a technical task? Navigation tree, article, contents, steps, code S07, S08 6 Course learning What should I learn, and what comes next? Learning routes, goals, duration, chapters, video and text S05, S06 7 Help knowledge base How do I resolve this problem? Search, topics, common questions, article collections S09 8 Developer publication What experience is worth reading? Categories, dates, authors, reading time, article rows S10"},{"title":"1 Company and brand","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#1-company-and-brand","text":"The company homepage begins with a large statement in mixed type, followed by highlighted content and article entrances. It establishes the company's focus before inviting a closer look at particular subjects. Design reading: the statement starts the narrative; selected content and article links extend it into a path from broad impression to specific topics."},{"title":"2 Product introduction","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#2-product-introduction","text":"The product page gives prominence to its topic, main actions, and demonstrations, then expands into capabilities and use. Task examples connect the explanation to recognizable situations. Claude product page with a large heading and scenario imagery Figure 5 · S02. The opening screen includes dynamic content; this is the captured frame. Design reading: the heading summarizes the use, scenarios show tasks and outcomes, and primary actions provide a way to continue or begin. Together they organize the opening screen."},{"title":"3 Pricing comparison","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#3-pricing-comparison","text":"Pricing after selecting Team and Enterprise, with the corresponding plan cards Figure 6 · S03. The Team & Enterprise selection displays the relevant plans side by side. Readers first choose Individual, Team & Enterprise, or API. Plan cards support an initial assessment, while the feature table supports detailed comparison. These layers answer different questions. Selecting an audience updates the selected tab and the displayed plans together. Interaction record"},{"title":"4 Resource library","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#4-resource-library","text":"Resources includes entrances for home, articles, guides, webinars, and videos, alongside search and filtering. Type, date, and reading information help readers judge relevance before opening the full content. Design reading: a rich opening screen supports exploration, while categories, search, and lists support readers arriving with a particular question."},{"title":"5 Technical documentation","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#5-technical-documentation","text":"Platform documentation with a navigation tree, steps and code, and page contents Figure 7 · S08. The desktop structure answers three questions at once: The left navigation shows where the article sits in the documentation. The central text explains how to complete the task. The right contents list shows the parts of the current article. Code Docs and Platform Docs share this reading structure, with different typographic and code details. Code Docs uses Paper Mono for code; Platform uses Anthropic Mono. A shared spatial arrangement accommodates distinct visual treatments."},{"title":"6 Course learning","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#6-course-learning","text":"Academy's homepage helps readers choose a learning route. A lesson combines goals, duration, video, summary or transcript, exercises, and subsequent content. It emphasizes sequence and learning objectives. Academy lesson with learning goals and chapter navigation Figure 8 · S06. Video content was not visible during capture; goals, text, and navigation were visible. Goals explain what the lesson covers, duration helps estimate the commitment, and chapter links establish the reading sequence. The transcript offers another learning entrance; see the interaction observations."},{"title":"7 Help knowledge base","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#7-help-knowledge-base","text":"The help center emphasizes search and topic collections, alongside common questions. Its organization supports direct entry into a problem. Design reading: search accepts a specific query, topic collections support browsing, and common questions provide direct entrances. These routes accommodate different levels of problem specificity."},{"title":"8 Developer publication","chapter":"Eight page patterns","href":"03-page-patterns.html#8-developer-publication","text":"The developer blog presents titles, dates, and reading time in rows, alongside topic filters and pixel graphics. The repeated rows support scanning and comparison. Selecting TUTORIALS narrows the list; returning to ALL restores it. The controls and rows form a path from choosing a topic to scanning titles and opening an article. Interaction record"},{"title":"Organizing components by task","chapter":"Twenty reusable patterns","href":"04-components.html#organizing-components-by-task","text":"The patterns form five groups of four: navigation, retrieval and switching, content presentation, comparison and steps, and learning and reading aids. Each serves a particular task: a card grid supports browsing; a plan card supports comparing offers."},{"title":"Navigation","chapter":"Twenty reusable patterns","href":"04-components.html#navigation","text":"ID Pattern Sample treatment Task P01 Top dropdown navigation Entrances grouped by products, developers, and resources Choose a direction across the site P02 Secondary category bar Articles, guides, and video entrances in Resources Switch categories within a content area P03 Breadcrumbs Parent locations in courses and documentation Understand hierarchy and return upward P04 Sidebar navigation and contents Documentation tree, article contents, lesson sections Locate and move within longer material Sources: resources S04, lesson S06, docs S07. P01, P02, and P04 handle site direction, the current category, and article position. Top navigation, category bars, and side regions give these different scales of orientation their own spaces."},{"title":"Retrieval and switching","chapter":"Twenty reusable patterns","href":"04-components.html#retrieval-and-switching","text":"ID Pattern Sample treatment Task P05 Site search Search entrances in resources, help, and docs Find target content directly P06 Category filtering Developer articles filtered by topic Narrow the candidate set P07 Tabs and segmented selection Pricing audiences, code platforms, or languages Switch related content in context P08 Expand and collapse FAQ entries and navigation groups Reveal detail as needed Sources: pricing S03, Code Docs S07, blog S10. State changes for P06 and P07 appear in the interaction record. Search accepts an open query, filtering constrains a collection, and tabs switch predefined content. Their controls can be compact while retaining these different behaviors."},{"title":"Content presentation","chapter":"Twenty reusable patterns","href":"04-components.html#content-presentation","text":"ID Pattern Sample treatment Task P09 Large-heading hero Heading, short explanation, and main action Establish the page's topic P10 Card grid Courses, product entrances, and resources Browse parallel choices P11 Article rows Dates, titles, and reading time arranged in rows Scan and compare articles P12 Product demonstration Visual scenes, tasks, and outcomes Explain how a capability is used Sources: product S02, resources S04, Academy S05, blog S10. Academy homepage with learning-route cards Figure 9 · S05. Each card combines a subject, description, and illustration to help readers choose a learning route. Cards suit items with distinct descriptions or imagery. Rows suit content with repeated fields that readers want to scan continuously. The choice follows content structure and the comparison task."},{"title":"Comparison and steps","chapter":"Twenty reusable patterns","href":"04-components.html#comparison-and-steps","text":"ID Pattern Sample treatment Task P13 Plan cards Name, price, action, and features grouped together Form an overall impression of a plan P14 Feature comparison table The same feature compared across plans Inspect decision-relevant differences P15 Numbered steps Setup and actions connected with sequence numbers Follow an execution order P16 Next lesson or next step A link to subsequent course content Continue after the current material Sources: pricing S03, lesson S06, Platform S08. Complete steps connect prerequisites, actions, and results. Useful comparison tables highlight differences that affect a choice. Numbers and tables give those relationships stable structures."},{"title":"Learning and reading aids","chapter":"Twenty reusable patterns","href":"04-components.html#learning-and-reading-aids","text":"ID Pattern Sample treatment Task P17 Video and text switching Lesson summary or transcript entrances Choose a learning format P18 Highlighted callout Goals or important explanations in their own group Establish expectations before reading P19 Code block and copy Commands or code beside a copy control Prepare content for execution P20 Duration and position cues Lesson numbers, duration, or section labels Judge commitment and current position Sources: lesson S06, Code Docs S07, Platform S08. Duration communicates commitment, chapter cues communicate position, and adjacent copy controls connect code reading with preparation to use it. These aids stay close to the text."},{"title":"Shared expression and local variation","chapter":"Twenty reusable patterns","href":"04-components.html#shared-expression-and-local-variation","text":"Recurring expression Changes with the task Dark primary actions and light secondary surfaces Corner shape, size, and arrangement of buttons and cards Clear heading and body hierarchy Serif or monospace heading choices Space or dividers between navigation and text Top, side, or collapsed navigation A visible distinction between code and prose Code font, language switching, and code-box details Type hierarchy, tonal relationships, and spatial grouping create consistency; corners, sizes, font choices, and navigation placement adapt to the page's purpose."},{"title":"Helping readers take the next step","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#helping-readers-take-the-next-step","text":"Visual hierarchy works alongside information structure. A large heading attracts attention, but readers also need a next action. A substantial article needs ways to locate sections and return to context. Situation Support in the samples Design reading First encounter with a product Brief description, scenarios, prominent main action Establish use before inviting action Comparing plans Audience selection, plan cards, detailed table Separate initial assessment from close comparison Finding technical content Navigation tree, search, page contents Support browsing and direct location Following instructions Numbered steps, code, nearby copy controls Keep explanation and execution material together Learning in sequence Goals, duration, next lesson Supply a starting point and a continuing route"},{"title":"Verified interactions","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#verified-interactions","text":"These results come from actions on public pages. See interaction-checks.json. Check Observed result Observed element Pricing audience selection Switching Individual to Team & Enterprise displays the corresponding plans Selected tab state and plan headings Blog topic filter The list changes from 10 items to 1, then returns to 10 with ALL Category selection and visible article count Lesson transcript selection Transcript becomes selected and the state updates Selected state of the content switch Documentation code font Sampled code renders with Paper Mono Text in the code area Resources first keyboard focus The first Tab reaches Skip to main content Keyboard entrance past navigation Academy after selecting the transcript, with chapter navigation alongside the text Figure 10 · Lesson S06. The video area was blank while loading during capture; the transcript control and lower text were available. This lesson offers video and text as two learning formats. Text and chapter navigation remain available while the video resource is not ready."},{"title":"Academy interactions","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#academy-interactions","text":"Nine interaction patterns were examined on 2026-11-03; eight inform this handbook. The strongest reference is Claude Academy: readers can see which cards are actionable, narrow a collection, choose a learning path, and keep their place inside a lesson. The follow-up used public pages in a fresh browser session, at 1440 × 1000 and 390 × 844. Action and measurement record Pattern What happened on Academy Decision for this handbook 1. Card hover On sampled product and course cards, the inner artwork scales to 1.04 over 300ms, with an ease-out curve; a subtle overlay appears. The text and outer frame stay in place. Adopt. Topic and chapter illustrations respond to hover and keyboard focus. Keep the title stable so the card remains easy to read. 2. Grouped menus Hovering Explore opens a three-column menu; Escape closes it. Resources has a smaller dropdown. Adopt. Explore groups the chapters by subject; Resources gathers the overview, comparison page, and evidence. Click and keyboard access accompany hover. 3. Global search The header search opens an overlay. A “Claude Code” query displayed six result options in the inspected state. Retain and refine. Search the handbook's sections in a dialog, with arrow-key navigation and Escape to return to the trigger. 4. Collection filtering In All resources, selecting Courses and searching “Claude Code” changed the cards, count, and address to ?kind=course&q=Claude+Code. The inspected course count changed from 28 to 12 results. Adapt. Four topic tabs and a chapter search update the cards, result count, and shareable address. Include a clear action and a useful empty state. 5. Guided entry In Get started, choosing Claude Code advances to a choice between learning to use it and rolling it out to a team. Simplify. Three illustrated starting points lead directly to matching chapters. A ten-chapter collection needs only one choice. 6. Theme switching The header theme control changes the page from light surfaces to dark surfaces. Adopt. Provide light and dark reading modes, remember the choice in the browser, and keep source screenshots in their original colors. 7. Content format switch In the AI Fluency lesson, Summary and Transcript form a radio group. Selecting Transcript changes both the selected state and the text. Adapt. The comparison page switches each sample between Screenshot and Analysis. This gives the same page two useful reading views. 8. Lesson orientation The lesson shows its position within the course, a chapter selector, section links, and a next-lesson entrance. At 390px, the chapter control opens a sheet containing the course list. Adapt. Articles show the current chapter and a desktop chapter selector alongside section links. The mobile chapter drawer and previous/next links retain orientation in a narrow layout. 9. Load more In the resource catalog, clicking Load more increased rendered resource cards from 24 to 48. Omit. Ten chapters fit in one collection. Filtering is useful; an additional reveal step is unnecessary."},{"title":"Menus and visible feedback","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#menus-and-visible-feedback","text":"Academy's Explore menu expanded into three grouped columns above the homepage Academy interaction capture A1 · Homepage · 1440 × 1000 · 2026-11-03. The expanded panel groups routes into separate columns while the page stays visible behind it. Card movement is deliberately small. Enlarging the illustration supplies a clear response without moving the heading or changing the card's footprint. In this handbook, the same response is available on keyboard focus, and the illustration stays still when the reader requests reduced motion. Menus use a short entrance transition; the selected topic has an underline; filtered cards make a brief, restrained entrance."},{"title":"A collection that explains its current state","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#a-collection-that-explains-its-current-state","text":"Academy's course catalog filtered by Claude Code, showing the selected category, query, result count, and matching cards Academy interaction capture A2 · Filtered catalog · 1440 × 1000 · 2026-11-03. The selected category, visible query, count, and cards describe the same state. This is more useful than an animated tab alone. The handbook carries that relationship into its chapter library: readers can copy a filtered address, refresh it, go back to a previous topic, or change language while keeping the filter. Clearing the controls restores all ten chapters."},{"title":"A reading surface with a second theme","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#a-reading-surface-with-a-second-theme","text":"Academy homepage in dark mode, with dark surfaces, light text, and colored product illustrations Academy interaction capture A3 · Homepage · 1440 × 1000 · 2026-11-03. Changing theme affects the reading surface, controls, and text hierarchy. Research images remain source material: the handbook displays their captured colors in both themes so that color comparisons continue to make sense."},{"title":"Course navigation on a narrow screen","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#course-navigation-on-a-narrow-screen","text":"Academy interaction capture A4 · AI Fluency lesson · 390 × 844 · 2026-11-03. The course list becomes an explicit overlay instead of compressing the reading column. The handbook uses its chapter drawer for the same job. On a wide screen, a compact chapter selector sits next to the article; section links below it track the current reading position. The next-chapter link keeps sequential reading available at the end of the page."},{"title":"Desktop and narrow viewports","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#desktop-and-narrow-viewports","text":"Academy: 390 × 844 Code Docs: 390 × 844 Figures 11–12 · Both captures use a desktop browser resized to a 390 × 844 viewport. Element Desktop, 1440px wide Narrow, 390px wide Academy main heading 48px, line height 52.8px 44px, line height 48.4px; wraps across lines Academy description 18px, line height 28px Remains 18px / 28px Academy content entrances Multiple card columns One vertical column with adjusted image/text relationships Code Docs main heading 36px, line height 40px 30px, line height 36px Code Docs introduction 16px, line height 26.4px Remains 16px / 26.4px Code Docs navigation Left navigation, central article, right contents One main reading column with compact navigation entrances Document-wide horizontal overflow None detected in either sample None detected in either sample Evidence: Academy desktop, Academy narrow, Code Docs desktop, Code Docs narrow. The narrow layout redistributes space: some headings shrink, body text retains its size, navigation becomes compact, and cards form a single column. Content priority drives the arrangement."},{"title":"Four text contrast checks","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#four-text-contrast-checks","text":"Contrast is calculated from the sampled text color and its nearest opaque ancestor background. The Resources hero sits on #F5F4ED, inside a page whose outer background is #FAF9F5; the calculation uses the panel behind the text. Sample Text Background Approximate contrast Resources heading #141413 #F5F4ED 16.72 : 1 Resources description #30302E #F5F4ED 11.99 : 1 Platform heading #0B0B0B #FCFCFB 19.17 : 1 Platform description #52514E #FCFCFB 7.73 : 1 W3C's minimum is 4.5 : 1 for normal text and 3 : 1 for qualifying large text, with specified exceptions. W3C contrast explanation All four pairs exceed the normal-text threshold. Both pages use dark headings and slightly lighter descriptions to establish hierarchy while keeping supporting text at a high contrast. Calculated data · Calculation script"},{"title":"Turning observations into design decisions","chapter":"UX & responsive layouts","href":"05-ux-and-responsive.html#turning-observations-into-design-decisions","text":"Design concern Concrete treatment Application State matches content Pricing selection updates the corresponding plans Keep selected styling aligned with displayed content Learning formats are available A transcript entrance accompanies the lesson Make alternate content easy to find Narrow layouts preserve reading priority Body text stays the same size as navigation collapses Arrange content order before adjusting dimensions Keyboard entry supports the main task Resources starts focus at a skip link Provide a direct route into the main content Hierarchy retains clarity Descriptions are lighter than headings but still high contrast Check both hierarchy and legibility"},{"title":"Ten representative pages","chapter":"Screenshot gallery","href":"06-gallery.html#ten-representative-pages","text":"S01 · Anthropic company homepage Anthropic company homepage The statement mixes Sans and Serif. The lower region shows a frame from dynamic content. Source · Measurements S02 · Claude product introduction Claude product introduction The heading, main actions, and scene imagery introduce product use. Source · Measurements S03 · Claude pricing Claude pricing in its Individual state The default Individual state organizes plan names, prices, descriptions, and main actions in columns. Source · Measurements S04 · Claude Resources Claude Resources A faint grid, large serif heading, search field, and article cards. Source · Measurements S05 · Claude Academy homepage Claude Academy homepage Learning entrances, course branding, and product cards. Source · Measurements S06 · Academy lesson Academy lesson page Learning goals, duration, and contents. Video content had not appeared during capture. Source · Measurements S07 · Claude Code documentation Claude Code documentation A desktop structure with left navigation, central text, and right page contents. Source · Measurements S08 · Claude Platform documentation Claude Platform documentation Numbered steps and code within a three-column reading structure. Source · Measurements S09 · Claude help center Claude help center in the captured font-fallback state Search and topic collections organize the page. During capture, headings and body text fell back to Times New Roman, and keyboard hints to Consolas. See the font records. Source · Measurements S10 · Claude developer blog Claude developer blog A monospace name, topic filter, pixel imagery, and article rows. Source · Measurements"},{"title":"Narrow viewports and interaction states","chapter":"Screenshot gallery","href":"06-gallery.html#narrow-viewports-and-interaction-states","text":"Academy at a narrow width Academy at a narrow width At a 390px-wide desktop-browser viewport, entrances form a single column, the heading wraps, and navigation contracts. Record Code Docs at a narrow width Code Docs at a narrow width Side navigation collapses and the article becomes the main reading area. Record Pricing: Team & Enterprise Team and Enterprise pricing selection The selected tab corresponds to the Team and Enterprise plans below. Record Lesson transcript selected Lesson with Transcript selected Transcript is selected. The blank video region reflects its loading state during capture. Record Code Docs code area Commands and copy controls in Code Docs Commands use Paper Mono, with copy controls and installation choices close to the code. Record"},{"title":"Original explanatory diagrams","chapter":"Screenshot gallery","href":"06-gallery.html#original-explanatory-diagrams","text":"Three visual directions Measured color samples Three approaches to assigning space These three diagrams organize visual directions, sampled colors, and spatial arrangements for comparison with the screenshots."},{"title":"Scope and date","chapter":"Methods & evidence","href":"07-methodology-and-sources.html#scope-and-date","text":"Observation date: 2026-11-02, Asia/Shanghai. The sample contains 10 representative pages across seven official sites. Academy home and Code Docs also have narrow captures; additional viewports and interaction states do not increase the page count. The scope includes public company, product, official learning, and documentation pages. It excludes the Claude conversation interface, signed-in pages, third-party tutorials, other language editions, and historical versions. Capture tools block access to claude.ai. An Academy interaction follow-up on 2026-11-03 adds nine inspected patterns and four original captures from its homepage, catalog, guided start, and lesson. It records card scaling and timing, menus, search, filtering, theme switching, and lesson controls. See the bilingual analysis and action record. These additional states are kept separately from the original ten-page sample, its 15 captures, and its classification counts."},{"title":"Source index","chapter":"Methods & evidence","href":"07-methodology-and-sources.html#source-index","text":"ID Sample Source Measurements S01 Anthropic company homepage Visit JSON S02 Claude product introduction Visit JSON S03 Pricing and comparison Visit JSON S04 Resource library Visit JSON S05 Academy homepage Visit JSON S06 Academy lesson Visit JSON S07 Claude Code documentation Visit JSON S08 Claude Platform documentation Visit JSON S09 Help center Visit JSON S10 Developer blog Visit JSON Screenshot content, prices, and product names retain their state on the observation date. Follow the source links for current information."},{"title":"Classification and evidence","chapter":"Methods & evidence","href":"07-methodology-and-sources.html#classification-and-evidence","text":"This handbook is an independent design analysis of public pages. Visual names and pattern categories are the study's framework; typography, color, dimensions, and interaction descriptions are supported by the records below. Type Content Basis Page facts Fonts, colors, structures, dimensions, and states after actions Original screenshots, browser measurements, interaction records Design interpretation Relationships between visual form and reading tasks Comparison and analysis of page facts Application advice Choices of layout, typography, and interaction for other projects Task-based suggestions in the application guide Three visual directions, eight page patterns, twenty component patterns, and font counts describe different levels and are counted independently. The classification covers inspected samples. Components are grouped by task: browsing cards and plan-comparison cards are separate; a pattern appearing on several sites is counted once. The count describes visible interface patterns rather than source-code components."},{"title":"Measurement and capture environment","chapter":"Methods & evidence","href":"07-methodology-and-sources.html#measurement-and-capture-environment","text":"Item Method Desktop 1440 × 1000 CSS pixels, device scale factor 1, light mode Narrow 390 × 844 CSS pixels, device scale factor 1; a resized desktop browser Page state Public, signed out, English content, normal motion preference Screenshots and diagrams Browser-generated PNG captures preserve the original scene; project-created SVGs explain structure or color Font styles Record family, size, weight, line height, declarations, and loading state Glyph rendering Read the fonts actually used by selected text elements Color Read element styles, interpreting transparency against ancestor backgrounds Dimensions Record the specific page, element, and viewport Font families can have static and variable versions and aliases with different capitalization or separators. The study groups identifiable families while retaining original names. Icon fonts, fallback fonts, and individual weights are separate from core text-family counts."},{"title":"Verification coverage and capture states","chapter":"Methods & evidence","href":"07-methodology-and-sources.html#verification-coverage-and-capture-states","text":"The table describes the original 2026-11-02 sample. The Academy follow-up above adds bounded interaction checks; its record specifies each action and result. Area Checked Not yet verified Interaction Pricing audience, blog category, and lesson transcript selection states Purchase and sign-in flows, video playback, saved progress, quizzes, search quality; code examples were not executed Responsive layout Two desktop/narrow pairs, text dimensions, document-wide overflow Physical-phone touch, other devices and breakpoints, all overlays and expanded states Accessibility Four text/background contrast pairs and the first Resources keyboard focus Full keyboard flows, target sizes, all interaction-state contrasts, site-wide WCAG auditing Motion and outcomes Visible dynamic scenes and relationships between structure and task Animation timing and triggers, reduced-motion behavior, user efficiency, conversion, and performance experiments Two capture states affect interpretation and are marked beside the corresponding figures: the support sample used fallback fonts, and lesson video content had not appeared while text remained available. Font and color records retain the specific elements and browser state."},{"title":"Reference standards","chapter":"Methods & evidence","href":"07-methodology-and-sources.html#reference-standards","text":"Contrast calculations follow the W3C explanation: 4.5 : 1 for normal text and 3 : 1 for qualifying large text, with specified exceptions. Calculations use sampled foreground colors and the nearest opaque ancestor background. For further control inspection, WCAG 2.2 AA's target-size criterion uses 24 × 24 CSS pixels with spacing and other exceptions. The table above defines the checks performed here."},{"title":"Updating the study","chapter":"Methods & evidence","href":"07-methodology-and-sources.html#updating-the-study","text":"Install Python and Playwright; source capture used Playwright 1.58.0 with Chrome. Run python scripts/capture.py --channel chrome from the repository root for desktop samples. Run python scripts/capture.py --mobile --ids S05 S07 --channel chrome for the two narrow samples. Run python scripts/check-interactions.py to refresh the recorded interactions. Inspect every screenshot, its loading state, and actual fonts; update both language editions. Run python scripts/analyze.py, python scripts/build_html.py, python scripts/validate.py, and python scripts/validate_html.py; update the observation date and change log when evidence changes. Recapture overwrites the corresponding evidence files, so use a separate branch. A source redesign may require updated selectors before its new results can be interpreted. HTML maintenance is described in the build guide."},{"title":"Start with roles, then choose appearance","chapter":"Application guide","href":"08-application-guide.html#start-with-roles-then-choose-appearance","text":"Build around a division of information: headings establish topics, body text explains value, contents help orientation, controls support action, and code or examples support execution. Then choose colors, typography, and decoration for the content and brand."},{"title":"Match the combination to the task","chapter":"Application guide","href":"08-application-guide.html#match-the-combination-to-the-task","text":"Your page Useful combination Decisions to make for your project Company introduction Clear statement, selected content, open composition Narrative tone, imagery, distinctive information Product introduction Clear heading, scenarios, prioritized main action Credible demonstrations, signed-out and signed-in routes, trial or purchase requirements Resource library Categories, search, topic labels, reading time Categories matching how readers seek content Technical docs Navigation tree, article, contents, code copying, steps Versions, runnable examples, error recovery, migrations Course Goals, duration, video and text, next step Exercises, progress, completion conditions, text alternatives Developer blog Compact rows, dates, categories, technical labels Whether retro imagery suits the audience and remains readable"},{"title":"Define your own typographic roles","chapter":"Application guide","href":"08-application-guide.html#define-your-own-typographic-roles","text":"Choose the roles before deciding how many fonts you need: Display and headings. Give them identity while supporting real title lengths and the target language. Reading and controls. Prioritize clear, stable body text, navigation, and buttons with suitable language coverage. Code and structured information. Use monospace for code, commands, and content requiring alignment. Check font licensing, loading cost, and character coverage together. See the materials notice for this project's asset scope. For Chinese headings, use real content to choose size: check length and character density, then refine line breaks, line height, and narrow layouts so headings remain proportionate to the body."},{"title":"Let color express hierarchy","chapter":"Application guide","href":"08-application-guide.html#let-color-express-hierarchy","text":"Use a small number of background levels, high-contrast primary text, and distinct roles for emphasis, states, and syntax. Links, warnings, decoration, and selection convey different meanings; color choices should make those meanings distinguishable. Check body text, secondary text, fine borders, and controls together, including hover, focus, and disabled states."},{"title":"Let the task determine density","chapter":"Application guide","href":"08-application-guide.html#let-the-task-determine-density","text":"Marketing pages can give a large area to one message. Knowledge pages need continuing orientation and a next step. Action-heavy tools need space for parallel information and repeated operations. Density should match task complexity. Sketch the reader's task sequence before assigning space: Task Information to show first Useful support Understand a product What it does and who it serves One or two credible examples Evaluate a plan Price and applicable scope Differences and conditions Complete installation Prerequisites and steps Platform choices and troubleshooting Learn a concept Goals and central explanation Examples, practice, next lesson Resolve a problem Search and topic categories Related questions and return routes"},{"title":"Review in a useful order","chapter":"Application guide","href":"08-application-guide.html#review-in-a-useful-order","text":"Content: Does the heading describe the page's purpose, and does the text explain necessary conditions? Structure: Do site navigation, categories, and article contents have distinct roles? Visual hierarchy: Do typography, spacing, borders, and color communicate clear levels? Interaction: Do search, switching, and expansion show state changes, with natural ways back? Narrow layouts: Is content ordered well, and do long headings or code crowd the page? Accessibility: Can the main flows be completed with a keyboard, with visible focus and clear text and controls? Reliability: Is content understandable when fonts, images, or video have not loaded? After the first draft, walk through the main tasks using real content. Check whether hierarchy, navigation, and state feedback support each step."},{"title":"Resources: from a content impression to search","chapter":"Page anatomy","href":"09-page-anatomy.html#resources-from-a-content-impression-to-search","text":"Resources with category navigation, peripheral articles, a central heading, and search Region Visible treatment Relationship to reading and action ① Two navigation levels Site-wide product and company links above resource categories Separates site direction from the current collection ② Peripheral article cards Titles, types, dates, or topics; some cards extend toward panel edges Suggests content breadth and gives browsing readers clues ③ Central heading and description A 72px serif heading with a 23px description and surrounding space Establishes a clear visual center ④ Search below the description An input with a keyboard-shortcut hint Connects understanding the page to finding content ⑤ Pale grid and borders A #F5F4ED panel with faint dividing lines Gives the cards, heading, and search a common surface Reading sequence: recognize the library → understand its range → browse an article or search. The center and peripheral cards support two routes: direct retrieval for a known question, or title scanning for exploration. Reusable combination: P02 category bar + P05 search + P09 large-heading hero + P10 card grid. For another library, establish its classification and retrieval entrances before choosing the visual weight of surrounding content. Source S04 · Font, size, and color measurements"},{"title":"Documentation: three scales of orientation","chapter":"Page anatomy","href":"09-page-anatomy.html#documentation-three-scales-of-orientation","text":"Code Docs with a navigation tree, central text, and page contents Region Visible treatment Reader's question ① Top tools Search, documentation categories, and site entrances Which documentation area should I enter? ② Left navigation Group headings and articles; a background highlights Overview Where am I in the documentation? ③ Central article Heading, introduction, Get started, and installation choices How do I begin this task? ④ Right contents Sections of the current article What does this article contain? ⑤ Platform and installation choices Terminal, VS Code, and other choices before their instructions Which instructions apply to my environment? The heading measures 36px and body text 16px. The heading sets hierarchy, the body supports continuous reading, and the two sides provide different scales of orientation. At the narrow viewport, the heading becomes 30px while body text remains 16px and navigation becomes compact."},{"title":"Alongside Platform documentation","chapter":"Page anatomy","href":"09-page-anatomy.html#alongside-platform-documentation","text":"Feature Claude Code documentation Claude Platform documentation Desktop structure Navigation tree, article, page contents Navigation tree, article, page contents Sampled main heading 36px / 40px line height 26.25px / 32.8125px line height Heading font Anthropic Serif Display Anthropic Serif Code font Paper Mono Anthropic Mono Getting-started structure Platform and installation-method choices Numbered steps and code examples Shared logic Keeps orientation, explanation, and execution entrances nearby Keeps orientation, explanation, and execution entrances nearby Reusable combination: P04 sidebar navigation and contents + P07 content switching + P19 code and copy. Platform also uses P15 numbered steps for its introductory flow. Code Docs S07 · Platform S08 · Code measurements · Platform measurements"},{"title":"Lessons: establish learning expectations first","chapter":"Page anatomy","href":"09-page-anatomy.html#lessons-establish-learning-expectations-first","text":"Academy with breadcrumbs, title, duration, a goals callout, and chapter navigation Video content was not visible during capture; the heading, learning goals, and section navigation were visible. Region Visible treatment Support for learning ① Breadcrumbs Academy, Courses, and the course title in a hierarchy Places a lesson within its course ② Title and duration Lesson 1 and 15 min below a serif title Identifies the subject, position, and expected commitment ③ Learning-goals callout An “In this lesson” group with separate goal rows Establishes expectations before the main material ④ Right-hand sections Current lesson, takeaways, exercises, and next steps Shows the sequence within the lesson ⑤ Content formats A video region and transcript selection Offers viewing and text-reading entrances Reading sequence: confirm course position → review goals and commitment → learn the material → review takeaways and exercises → continue. Duration and goals help readers decide whether the lesson fits their current needs. Reusable combination: P03 breadcrumbs + P18 callout + P20 duration and position, connected by P17 format switching and P16 next-lesson navigation. Source S06 · Measurements · Transcript check"},{"title":"How the three pages allocate attention","chapter":"Page anatomy","href":"09-page-anatomy.html#how-the-three-pages-allocate-attention","text":"Page Establishes first Orientation Next action Resources Content range and topic Categories, search, cards Choose content Documentation Current technical task Navigation tree, contents, platform choices Follow steps and use code Lesson Learning goals and commitment Course hierarchy and sections Learn, practice, continue Shared type and surface colors produce different rhythms in these structures. Start with the reader's route, then choose heading proportions, navigation levels, and action entrances to support it."},{"title":"Reading tasks across ten samples","chapter":"Cross-site matrix","href":"10-cross-site-matrix.html#reading-tasks-across-ten-samples","text":"Sample Main task Spatial organization Identifying detail S01 Company Understand the company's focus Large statement and selected content Sans and Serif within one main heading S02 Product Recognize product uses Heading, main actions, scenario demonstrations Task and outcome imagery S03 Pricing Choose a suitable plan Audience selection, plan cards, comparison Parallel plans and clear action entrances S04 Resources Browse or find content Categories, search, cards Pale grid, large serif heading, line illustrations S05 Academy home Choose a learning route Learning topics and cards Product imagery and course names S06 Lesson Complete a unit of learning Goals, material, sections, subsequent links Duration labels and a goals callout S07 Code Docs Start using a developer tool Three reading columns and installation choices Serif Display headings, Paper Mono code S08 Platform Docs Complete technical setup Three reading columns, steps, code Compact headings and Anthropic Mono S09 Help center Resolve a problem Search, topic collections, common questions Problem-oriented entrances S10 Developer blog Scan technical articles Topic filtering and article rows Monospace labels and pixel graphics Page URLs and records appear in the source index."},{"title":"Typographic roles compared","chapter":"Cross-site matrix","href":"10-cross-site-matrix.html#typographic-roles-compared","text":"Context Headings or identity Reading and controls Technical material Company Mixed Sans and Serif statement Anthropic Sans Primarily brand narrative Resources Anthropic Serif Anthropic Sans Card metadata and retrieval controls Academy Anthropic Serif Anthropic Sans Duration, chapters, and learning cues Code Docs Anthropic Serif Display Anthropic Sans Paper Mono code Platform Docs Anthropic Serif Anthropic Sans Anthropic Mono code Developer blog Anthropic Mono site name Anthropic Sans article titles and body Anthropic Mono metadata The help center is recorded separately: it declares Anthropic Sans, Anthropic Serif, and local JetBrains Mono, while sampled text fell back to Times New Roman and Consolas. See the typography chapter."},{"title":"Recorded pattern distribution","chapter":"Cross-site matrix","href":"10-cross-site-matrix.html#recorded-pattern-distribution","text":"The table lists verified representative samples for each pattern. One pattern can occur on several pages. The HTML edition also offers filters by task group. Pattern Representative samples Consider when combining P01 Top dropdown navigation S01, S04 Grouping site entrances P02 Secondary category bar S04 Current content scope P03 Breadcrumbs S06, S08 Hierarchy and return paths P04 Sidebar navigation and contents S06, S07, S08 Site position and article position P05 Site search S04, S07, S09 Direct location of target content P06 Category filtering S10 Constraining the article collection P07 Tabs and segmented selection S03, S07, S08 Matching selection to displayed content P08 Expand and collapse S03, S07 Revealing detail as needed P09 Large-heading hero S01, S02, S04 Page topic and primary action P10 Card grid S04, S05 Scannable parallel content P11 Article rows S10 Alignment of titles and metadata P12 Product demonstration S02 Connecting scenarios to use P13 Plan cards S03 Overall impressions of options P14 Feature comparison table S03 Differences across plans P15 Numbered steps S08 Prerequisites, actions, results P16 Next lesson or next step S06 Continuing from the current content P17 Video and text switching S06 Learning-format choices P18 Highlighted callout S06 Goals and key conditions P19 Code block and copy S07, S08 Proximity of explanation and execution material P20 Duration and position cues S05, S06 Commitment and current position Sources: taxonomy · component descriptions."},{"title":"Choose a combination for your page","chapter":"Cross-site matrix","href":"10-cross-site-matrix.html#choose-a-combination-for-your-page","text":"Content condition Useful combination Content preparation Many items and varied reader intentions Category bar + search + cards or rows Clear types, titles, and summaries Actions depend on earlier steps Contents + numbered steps + code copying Prerequisites, results, troubleshooting Several options need comparison Audience selection + plan cards + comparison Applicable scope, differences, selection conditions Learning follows a sequence Goals + duration + sections + next lesson Objectives, exercises, a continuing route Choose the task combination before adjusting typography, color, and density. This turns the observations into a purposeful interface proposal."}];
